*is this a commitment I can put in writing, or just \"we're evaluating\"?*\n\n**2. Whether to surface the Q2 history.**\nThe ticket notes this cert was flagged before and never got renewal approval. I omitted that entirely from the client email — a judgment call I made silently. That's a liability-adjacent decision (does silence look like you're hiding it, or does mentioning it open a \"so this was preventable and you didn't tell us\" conversation?). You know your relationship with this client and your MSA better than I ever will — tell me whether that history is in-bounds for the email or strictly internal.\n\n**3. Length and register.**\n\"Apologize and let them know we're on top of it\" reads like you wanted something short — a few sentences, not a mini-RCA with a timeline. I defaulted to full incident-report mode because I had all the ticket detail in front of me and used it. If you want brief, say \"keep it to a paragraph\" or \"no technical detail, just reassurance.\"\n\n**4. Audience.**\nI addressed it to Dana. Is she the only recipient, or does this also go to the practice owner/dentist, who might want a different level of detail than the office manager?\n\n**5. Whether there's a make-good.**\nP1, full outage, multiple sites — some MSPs offer an SLA credit or comp in this situation. If that's on the table, it belongs in the email; if not, worth confirming so I don't invent or omit it wrong.\n\nThe general pattern: when you hand me a ticket and say \"email the client,\" I'll fill every gap with the most complete, defensible version I can construct from the ticket data — which is often *more* disclosure and *more* commitment than you actually want to put in writing. The fastest fix isn't a longer prompt every time; it's flagging the 1-2 things that are actually decisions (commitment level, what to disclose, length) rather than facts I can just read off the ticket.","ttft_ms":15003,"type":"result","duration_ms":25335,"uuid":"cc24c63e-44bd-4d42-88c8-8912273ad8ac